Stop libkineto's global-scope instant events from spawning Unnamed SliceTracks.

Two emitters produce instant events that Perfetto cannot route to a named
track:

1. The singleton "Record Window End" marker carries empty-string pid/tid and
   global scope ("s":"g"). Perfetto treats empty pid/tid as an unrouted event
   and global scope as a process-wide instants track, so the marker spawns an
   "Unnamed SliceTrack" in every trace. Emit it on the (-1, 0) overhead track
   (which carries a process_name) with thread scope instead.

2. The per-CpuTraceBuffer "Iteration Start: <span>" marker
   (addIterationMarker) is emitted with global scope. Its pid/tid strings
   ("Traces" / "Trace <span>") name a perfectly good track, but global scope
   routes it to the unnamed process-wide instants track anyway. Demote it to
   thread scope so it lands on its own named track. This fires for every
   client CPU-trace transfer (MAX's semantic-range buffer, PyTorch's op
   buffer alike).

MAX previously fixed the first case by text-patching the trace file after
writing it (Range.cpp::patchOverheadTrackMetadata), but that only covers
traces written through M::Profiling::disable(); Dynolog on-demand traces are
serialized by libkineto itself and bypassed the fixup (MXTOOLS-266). Patching
the emitters covers every write path.

--- a/libkineto/src/output_json.cpp
+++ b/libkineto/src/output_json.cpp
@@ -605,7 +605,7 @@
   writeInstantEvent(
       /*cat=*/"",
       /*name=*/fmt::format("Iteration Start: {}", span.name),
-      /*scope=*/"g",
+      /*scope=*/"t",
       /*pid=*/R"("Traces")",
       /*tid=*/fmt::format("\"Trace {}\"", span.name),
       /*ts=*/start,
@@ -945,9 +945,9 @@
   writeInstantEvent(
       /*cat=*/"",
       /*name=*/"Record Window End",
-      /*scope=*/"g",
-      /*pid=*/R"("")",
-      /*tid=*/R"("")",
+      /*scope=*/"t",
+      /*pid=*/"-1",
+      /*tid=*/"0",
       /*ts=*/endTime,
       /*args=*/emptyArgs,
       /*finalEvent=*/true);
